> looks like there was more than just KEY's done

Regression from #49281, seems like the same issue, `MouseButton` is another enum used in bitwise operations.

Maybe we should change all `enum`s to `enum class`es:

```c++
enum Type {
  A = 0, B = 1, C = 2
};

Type x = (Type)((int)A | (int)B); // UB ⛔️ <- This is situation after #47378 and #49281
int x = (int)A | (int)B; // OK <- This was a situation before aforementioned PRs
```

```c++
enum class Type {
  A = 0, B = 1, C = 2
};

Type x = (Type)((int)A | (int)B); // OK <- That's what probably should be done.
int x = (int)A | (int)B; // OK
```

```cpp
enum MouseButton {
	MOUSE_BUTTON_NONE = 0,
	MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT = 1,
	MOUSE_BUTTON_RIGHT = 2,
};
MouseButton mb = MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT;
```
vs
```cpp
enum class MouseButton {
	NONE = 0,
	LEFT = 1,
	RIGHT = 2,
};
MouseButton mb = MouseButton::LEFT;
```
I think the `enum class` code is nicer anyway, even without the fact that it may help us fix a bug.

> Are there disadvantages to enum class?

I do not think there are disadvantages. But, it's gonna need a lot of code changes to implement, name prefixes everywhere (e.g. `MouseButton::BUTTON_1` instead of `BUTTON_1`), and explicit casts to `int` and back if it's assigned to `int` variable and every time a bitwise operation is used, something like:
```c++
inline Type operator | (Type a, Type b) {
    using T = std::underlying_type_t <Type>;
    return static_cast<Type>(static_cast<T>(a) | static_cast<T>(b));
}
```

